A||||7||DjKid||Motor Mount.. replacement!||||5||
Z||000000||DjKid||09-25-2000||08:59 PM||||First of all let me say, I am very pleased with the price of the motor mount, 25$ not bad in my mind.  and the parts store actually had one in stock, shocked me! anyways, heres my question... could someone explain to me how to remove the old motor mount and reinstall the new one without removing the engine&cradle... hopefully without having to lower the cradle any at all. TIA <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Ray - Blue 88 Coupe<BR><A HREF="http://blueknight.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank>My Blue Knight</A> - <A HREF="http://www.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank> Carolina Fieros</A>||||reg||5
Z||000001||SCCA FIERO||09-25-2000||10:16 PM||||Unless I am missing something here... it's simple. Just unbolt the 4 bolts on the mount, jack up the engine, and remove the mount.<P>------------------<BR>-Chris <BR>88 Fiero Coupe<BR><B>KEEP THE EXCITEMENT ALIVE!!</B>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||09-26-2000||02:52 AM||||Use a 2x4 or something similar so you don't dent the oil pan to support the engine with a jack.  Lift it up a little to take the weight off the mount, unbolt the old one and slip the new one in.<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 150,000 miles and counting!       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">    <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||1
Z||000003||DjKid||09-26-2000||04:57 AM||||I could use a lift to lift the engine from above couldn't I?<P>------------------<BR>Ray - Blue 88 Coupe<BR><A HREF="http://blueknight.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank>My Blue Knight</A> - <A HREF="http://www.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank> Carolina Fieros</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-26-2000||08:37 AM||||Doesn't matter how you lift it as long as you are careful not to break anything. You should be able to spread the brackets 2-4 inches as long as you are easy on the wires and hoses.<P>disconect the battery just to be safe.||||reg||1
Z||000005||the need for speed||09-27-2000||01:26 AM||||when i changed my oil it looks like my oil pan has a dint in it, it was there since a got the car.Is the oil pan flat on the bottom,the car runs ok. Will this create a problem .or are you talking about not crushing the pan with all that weight meaning a dint is not a problem?||||reg||1
Z||000006||ur2biteme||09-27-2000||04:27 AM||||it may be different, but i recently had to replace the engine mount bracket on my 87 (the a.c. mounting bracket and bolts decided they didn't want to be on the engine anymore, snapped the other side, decided to put a hole in my oil filter for me......) i know the engines are really close, but i'm not sure of the engine mounts.  i think they are close enough for this to work. this is the ordeal i went through:<P>1:  i unbolted all the bolts on the old bracket to the mount<P>2:  unbolted the dogbone<P>3:  raised the engine with a floor jack (nothing in between the jack and the oil pan in my case, but it's a good idea.  i don't know about the '88s, but the 87 has a particular spot on the pan if you put a jack there, it doesn't budge at all)<P>4:  raised the engine as far as my concience would allow me to, being that i wanted to keep my oil pan, but about twice the gap for the mount.<P>     i'll save my cursing and yelling and <BR>     busted knuckles replacing the bracket<P>5:  once the bracket was in, i put the mount back in place<P>6:  lowered the engine enough to line up the studs with the holes<P>7:  lowered the engine just enough to get the nuts on the studs loosely<P>8:  lowered the engine down the rest of the way and tightened up the nuts.<P>i've gotta do this again soon.  ANOTHER snapped engine mount (i think someone swapped in a detuned super duty.  cant explain the mount snapping torque any other way  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  )  between all the mounts i've replaced them about 9 times!  my own stupidity, i know what i've been doing wrong(gotta make sure that ALL the mounts are good, and don't drive over center dividers), but at least i know how to replace the darn things now.  hope this helps.  <P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>169k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000007||DjKid||09-27-2000||05:54 AM||||Thats what I was lookin for  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> thank you. I'll prolly be doing this myself  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> with no help from my brother, so all the fun is mine. Anyways.. thnx to all ya'll for helping! btw, I hate my keyboard hehe!<P>------------------<BR>Ray - Blue 88 Coupe<BR><A HREF="http://blueknight.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank>My Blue Knight</A> - <A HREF="http://www.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank> Carolina Fieros</A>||||reg||1
